# Engineering and agent configuration

## Choose between overlapping skills

- Implementation: `ponytail` is the required code-change baseline. Add a domain specialist for the actual work; use `diagnosing-bugs` when something is broken and `tdd` when test-first work is requested.
- Reviews: `ponytail-review` checks a change for correctness, safety, performance, and unnecessary complexity; `code-review` compares changes against documented Standards and the originating Spec from a fixed base; `ponytail-audit` examines a whole repository. Choose the requested review scope before loading a reviewer.
- Architecture: `codebase-design` addresses module boundaries and interfaces; `domain-modeling` addresses domain language, glossary, and ADRs. `reverse-engineer-anything` concerns shipped artifacts and runtime evidence, rather than an ordinary source review.
- Agent configuration: `writing-for-agents` is for AGENTS.md and other instruction documents; `skill-creator` adds skill packaging and trigger design; `skill-installer` installs skills; `openai-docs` checks Codex/OpenAI product behavior. A plain routing README needs no new wrapper skill.
- Special workflows: `vibe-wise` is for requested learning during implementation, `grilling` for requested stress-testing, and `wizard` for user-only setup steps. Use `sol-loop` when named. Explicit planning and teaching flows are in [explicit-only skills](explicit.md).

