   1: # Memory handoff: what the claims need, and what we have (2026-11-07)
   2: 
   3: Written at the researcher's request after the v8 result, when five simple questions showed that "the memory goal is met" did not match the memory the researcher's claims need. This file replaces nothing. The [2026-11-04 memory goal](memory-goal-2026-11-04.md) and every run, report and gate stay unchanged as history. Read this file first before any new memory work.
   4: 
   5: ## 1. The memory the claims need
   6: 
   7: The researcher's words: "a memory that supports learning, so that it can learn useful memories that will be stored as experience." Their later questions added three requirements: no overwriting, no clues, and memory that can feed reasoning. Together, these are four properties.
   8: 
   9: | # | Property | Plain meaning | Needed for |
  10: |---|---|---|---|
  11: | Property 1 | **In the fly's own connections** | Learning changes connection strengths inside the modelled fly circuit, through a biologically justified rule driven by a teaching signal through the modelled teaching pathway (dopamine neurons). Not a separate store. | Vol. 2 |
  12: | Property 2 | **Lasts while the fly keeps learning** | Learn A, then learn B, then test A: A still works. Also new learning and correction still work. Measured under capacity pressure, with overwrites reported. | Vol. 2 (pass) or Vol. 4 (proven limit) |
  13: | Property 3 | **Helps on new positions, with no clues** | On boards from games never used in practice, choices are better than with no learning and with shuffled feedback. The fly chooses among **all legal moves**; no filter tells it that a safe answer is in the list. Feedback reaches it only through its own teaching pathway. | Vol. 2 |
  14: | Property 4 | **Stores lessons, not only cases** | It keeps general rules (for example "taking a defended piece is risky") that can be combined on an unfamiliar kind of position, where no similar case was stored. | Vol. 3 |
  15: 
  16: **Three levels of "new"** (Property 3 versus Property 4): (1) the same board again: memorisation; (2) a new board, the same kind of situation: recall of similar cases (Property 3); (3) a new kind of situation: combining lessons (Property 4, the researcher's definition of reasoning: "being able to apply many unrelated concepts to solve an unfamiliar problem").
  17: 
  18: **Allowed aids, if disclosed:** an engineered memory is allowed as a labelled computational aid for Vol. 3 (researcher, 2026-11-01, [decision log](../../../research/project/decision_log.md)). It does not count towards Property 1 or Property 2. Any reasoning result that uses it must beat a lookup-only control, so that the fly, not the store, is shown to do the work.
  19: 
  20: ## 2. What we have (all preserved)
  21: 
  22: ### 2a. The fly's own connections (Property 1, Property 2)
  23: 
  24: | Date | Test | Result | Evidence |
  25: |---|---|---|---|
  26: | 2026-10-31 | memory-plasticity fixtures | failed candidates; a proven conflict for the bounded reader | [report](../../../research/reports/overnight-memory-plasticity-2026-10-31/MORNING-REPORT.md) |
  27: | 2026-10-31 | retention continuation | no joint pass; the prediction-scaled pulse passes 19 of 28 development cells, not selected | [report](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-10-31/README.md) |
  28: | 2026-10-31 | eye-graph memory v1 | retention 0.52, gate 0.70: **fails** | [report](../../../research/reports/memory-retention-continuation-2026-11-01/README.md) |
  29: | 2026-11-01 | cubic-exit comparison | 3 of 6 informative seeds **lose an unrelated old memory** after reversal | same report |
  30: | 2026-11-01 | interference diagnosis | cause in seed 4102: six shared active connections; restoring them saves the old memory but breaks the reversal | [report](../../../research/reports/memory-interference-diagnosis-2026-11-01/README.md) |
  31: | 2026-11-02 | interference limit | certificate for that one seed: no reset of the shared connections to their resting values keeps both memories | [report](../../../research/reports/interference-limits-2026-11-02/README.md) |
  32: | 2026-11-03 | memory test v1 (Î³4 rule, chess) | identical in every arm, including learning off; design mistakes: the rewarded move matched the built-in preference, and visual input drives PAM08 | [report](../../../research/reports/memory-test-2026-11-03/README.md) |
  33: 
  34: **State:** Property 1 exists only in development tests with non-chess associations. **Property 2 fails**: overwriting is measured, its cause is located in one seed, and that seed has a bounded impossibility proof. No stable rule is selected. No chess test of the fly's own learning has worked.
  35: 
  36: ### 2b. The engineered store (Property 3 only)
  37: 
  38: | Version | Result | What it taught |
  39: |---|---|---|
  40: | v1 | never run | design faults (oldest-match wins; wrong random control) |
  41: | v2 | fails | the Kenyon-cell code shrinks the deciding one-piece difference 48 times |
  42: | v3 | fails | ties and unequal practice decided the scores |
  43: | v4 | fails | raw and random keys learn; the fly's key barely does |
  44: | v5 | fails | a position-centred fly key helps, but more experience does not |
  45: | v6 | passes (audit pending) | **shortcut**: capture against quiet move; below random capture (131-145 v 250) |
  46: | v7 | fails (1 seed) | only about 300 relevant experiences per seed |
  47: | v8 | **passes, audit PASS** | meets the 2026-11-04 goal; see the limits below |
  48: 
  49: **v8 limits, found by the researcher's questions on 2026-11-07:**
  50: 
  51: 1. **Filtered choice (clue).** The fly chooses only among captures whose safety the checker knows. Each test position was selected to contain both a safe and an unsafe capture. Among **all legal moves**, v8 scores 30.6 of 560 against 21.9 for random: almost no help.
  52: 2. **Not in the fly's connections.** Connections never change. Each experience has its own slot, so overwriting cannot happen. This avoids Property 2; it does not solve it.
  53: 3. **Retention is trivial.** The store (4,096) was never full (2,509); the test is exact-key recall of a stored result.
  54: 4. **The fly adds nothing measurable.** Raw input (383) and random features (363) beat the fly's key (349) with the same store.
  55: 5. **Feedback skips the fly.** The checker writes "safe" or "unsafe" into the store; no teaching signal reaches the modelled pathway.
  56: 6. **Case recall, not lessons.** Nearest-match lookup; level 2 of "new" only.
  57: 7. **Exploratory.** Eight versions designed against the same gate; the seeds share one set of experiences.
  58: 
  59: ### 2c. What is known to work
  60: 
  61: 1. The eyes deliver the board to the memory cells (seeing test v1: knight against bishop, 48/48).
  62: 2. Verified safe/unsafe certificates, a fair test pipeline (unseen games, board exclusion, s / c ties, game-clustered bootstrap) and an independent auditor all exist and are audited.
  63: 3. Verified experience contains information that improves choices on new boards (v8, filtered).
  64: 
  65: ## 3. The gap
  66: 
  67: | Property | Have | Missing |
  68: |---|---|---|
  69: | Property 1 | development tests only | a chess test where the fly's own connections learn through its teaching pathway |
